diskfont.library/AvailFonts                        diskfont.library/AvailFonts

   NAME
	AvailFonts -- Every font in memory and in FONTS:, described.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn AvailFonts(dfb: *DiskfontBase, buffer: *anyopaque, buffer_size: u32, flags: u32) u32

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -24.

   INPUTS
	- `buffer` - where the answer goes, four-byte aligned.
	- `buffer_size` - how many bytes it has.
	- `flags` - where to look: `AFF_MEMORY` for the fonts on graphics'
	  list, `AFF_DISK` for every size every contents file in `FONTS:`
	  lists, `AFF_SCALED` to count memory fonts made by scaling as well.

   RESULT
	0 when everything fitted: the buffer holds an `AvailFontsHeader`, its
	`count` `AvailFonts` entries (`diskfont.availEntries`), and the names
	they point at. Otherwise
	how many bytes more it needed; the buffer is then not filled, and a
	caller tries again with that much more.

   BEHAVIOR
	Each entry is the `TextAttr` that opens the font - with `OpenFont` for
	one in memory, `OpenDiskFont` for one on a disk - and where it was:
	`AFF_MEMORY`, `AFF_MEMORY | AFF_SCALED`, `AFF_DISK`, or
	`AFF_DISK | AFF_SCALABLE` with a height of 0 for an outline, which
	comes at any height. A font loaded
	from a disk is listed under both, once for each place. A disk entry
	says what its contents file says; whether the size file itself is
	sound is only known when it is opened. Every directory of a `FONTS:`
	assign of several is looked in.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: yes: for the disk, and for graphics' font list.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: no spinlock may be held.
	- Process: a Process, since it reads files.

   OWNERSHIP
	The buffer is the caller's; the names in it point into it.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`OpenDiskFont`, `graphics.library/NextFont`

   EXAMPLES
	    var size: u32 = 1024;
	    while (true) {
	        const buffer = sys.AllocVec(size, exec.MEMF_ANY) orelse return;
	        const more = dfb.AvailFonts(buffer, size, diskfont.AFF_MEMORY | diskfont.AFF_DISK);
	        if (more == 0) break use(buffer);
	        sys.FreeVec(buffer);
	        size += more;
	    }
diskfont.library/CloseOutlineFont            diskfont.library/CloseOutlineFont

   NAME
	CloseOutlineFont -- What OpenOutlineFont gave, given back.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn CloseOutlineFont(dfb: *DiskfontBase, font: ?*diskfont.OutlineFont) void

   SINCE
	1.4. LVO -44.

   INPUTS
	- `font` - what `OpenOutlineFont` answered, or null.

   RESULT
	Nothing.

   BEHAVIOR
	One holder fewer; null does nothing. The last one's close leaves the
	outline loaded, for the next to ask for it, until memory runs short.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: yes: for another task loading a font.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: no spinlock may be held.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The outline is no longer the caller's to read.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`OpenOutlineFont`

   EXAMPLES
	    dfb.CloseOutlineFont(face);
diskfont.library/DisposeFontContents      diskfont.library/DisposeFontContents

   NAME
	DisposeFontContents -- Frees what NewFontContents made.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn DisposeFontContents(dfb: *DiskfontBase, contents: ?*fontfile.ContentsHeader) void

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -32.

   INPUTS
	- `contents` - what `NewFontContents` answered, or null.

   RESULT
	Nothing.

   BEHAVIOR
	The image goes back to the system; null does nothing.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The image is gone; nothing may read it after.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`NewFontContents`

   EXAMPLES
	    dfb.DisposeFontContents(made);
diskfont.library/NewFontContents              diskfont.library/NewFontContents

   NAME
	NewFontContents -- A family's contents file, made from its directory.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn NewFontContents(dfb: *DiskfontBase, lock: ?*dos.FileLock, name: [*:0]const u8) ?*fontfile.ContentsHeader

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -28.

   INPUTS
	- `lock` - the directory the family is in: `FONTS:`, or wherever its
	  contents file is to go.
	- `name` - the family's name, ending in ".font": "spleen.font". Its
	  sizes are in the directory of that name without ".font".

   RESULT
	The contents file's image, sealed and ready to be written as
	`<name>`; null if the name does not end in ".font" or is too long, the
	directory cannot be read, it holds no font, or there is no memory.

   BEHAVIOR
	Every file in the family's directory that is a whole font - its image
	checks and its sum is sound - becomes an entry: its path from `lock`
	("spleen/16"), height, drawn styles, flags, kind and width, as its own
	header says. A TrueType or OpenType file of one font becomes the
	family's outline entry, of height 0, in the styles the file says it
	is drawn in - bold for a bold face. Anything else there is passed
	over. The entries are in order of height. At most 64 sizes are
	listed.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: yes, for the disk.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: no spinlock may be held.
	- Process: a Process, since it reads files.

   OWNERSHIP
	The image is the caller's, freed with `DisposeFontContents`. Its size
	is `fontfile.contentsSize(header.count)`. `lock` stays the caller's.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`DisposeFontContents`, `AvailFonts`

   EXAMPLES
	    const made = dfb.NewFontContents(fonts_lock, "spleen.font") orelse return;
	    defer dfb.DisposeFontContents(made);
	    _ = dl.Write(fh, made, @intCast(fontfile.contentsSize(made.count)));
diskfont.library/NewScaledDiskFont          diskfont.library/NewScaledDiskFont

   NAME
	NewScaledDiskFont -- A font of another height, made from one there is.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn NewScaledDiskFont(dfb: *DiskfontBase, font: *TextFont, text_attr: *const TextAttr) ?*TextFont

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -36.

   INPUTS
	- `font` - the font to scale from: any kind, ink, coverage or colour.
	- `text_attr` - the height wanted (`y_size`); the rest is not used.

   RESULT
	The new font, on no list and open to nobody, or null for a height of
	0, a height at which a glyph's box, row or advance is more than a
	font image holds (32767 pixels, 65535 bytes a row), or no memory.

   BEHAVIOR
	Every glyph is scaled by the ratio of the heights, across as much as
	down, so a fixed-width font stays fixed-width and letters keep their
	shape. Each pixel of the new glyph takes the value of the pixel of the
	old one under its middle - nearest, not blended, so ink stays ink,
	coverage keeps its steps, palette entries stay palette entries and a
	pixel of 32-bit colour keeps its colour and coverage. The boxes, the
	advances, the width and the baseline scale with them, rounded. Glyphs
	several characters share are scaled once.

	The new font has the old one's name, styles and palette, and is not
	`FPF_DESIGNED`: it was made, not drawn.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The caller's: one allocation, freed with `FreeVec(font)` - after
	`RemFont` if the caller put it on graphics' list with `AddFont`.
	`font` is only read.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`OpenDiskFont`, `graphics.library/AddFont`

   EXAMPLES
	    const big = dfb.NewScaledDiskFont(font, &.{ .name = "", .y_size = 48 }) orelse return;
	    defer sys.FreeVec(big);
diskfont.library/OpenDiskFont                    diskfont.library/OpenDiskFont

   NAME
	OpenDiskFont -- The nearest font to what is asked for, loaded or scaled as needed.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn OpenDiskFont(dfb: *DiskfontBase, text_attr: *const TextAttr) ?*TextFont

   SINCE
	1.0, fallback fonts since 1.8, an outline's glyphs made when
	wanted since 1.9. LVO -20.

   INPUTS
	- `text_attr` - the font's name ("spleen.font", or a path to its
	  contents file), height - in rows, or in points with `FPF_POINTS` -
	  style and flags. A height of 0 is 1.

   RESULT
	The font, open: close it with graphics' `CloseFont`. Null if there is
	no font of the name, or none that will do - `FPF_DESIGNED` asked for
	and no size drawn.

   BEHAVIOR
	**A font in memory that matches perfectly is answered at once.**

	**Bold and italic are the family's own faces where it has them.**
	Asked for `FSF_BOLD` or `FSF_ITALIC`, the faces installed beside the
	family - `go-bolditalic.font`, `go-bold.font`, `go-italic.font` beside
	`go.font`, each only for styles asked - are tried first, the one
	drawing the most of them first, and opened as the families they are.
	A face is drawn in its styles, so graphics' soft styles draw only what
	it lacks: bold-italic asked of a family with only a bold face is that
	face, leaned.

	Otherwise the family's contents file is read - `FONTS:<name>`, or the
	name as given when it has a ':' - and:

	- **A family with an outline** (a TrueType or OpenType file) takes a
	  size drawn at exactly the height and style asked when it has one,
	  then a size
	  of the outline already in memory at that height, and otherwise has
	  truetype.library render the outline at that height - at any height,
	  and with `FPF_DESIGNED` too, since it is drawn and not scaled. A
	  size rendered is in the styles the outline is drawn in, as its entry
	  says. Its image holds Latin, Greek, Cyrillic, punctuation, currency,
	  the letterlike symbols and box drawing; any other character the
	  outline has a glyph for is rendered the first time it is drawn or
	  measured, and kept with the size (`TextFont.render`).
	- **Without `FPF_DESIGNED`**, a source is looked for at the height
	  asked, then twice it, then half it (when even), in memory first and
	  then among the family's sizes, the style asked for or one with
	  underline, then bold, then italic let go, since the soft styles can
	  draw those. Failing all of that, the nearest size by `WeighTAMatch`.
	  A source of another height is scaled to the height asked
	  (`NewScaledDiskFont`), and the scaled font kept like a loaded one.
	- **With `FPF_DESIGNED`**, the nearest size drawn, by `WeighTAMatch`,
	  whether in memory or on the disk: never a scaled one.

	A size read from the disk must be a whole font file (checked and
	sound); one that is not is passed over for the next best. Fonts are
	loaded one at a time, whoever asks.

	**A font made here is given its fallback fonts** (`TextFont.fallback`):
	the families `ENV:Sys/font.prefs` names under `FALLBACK`, each opened
	at the font's own height as this call opens a family without
	`FPF_DESIGNED` - loaded, rendered or scaled - and chained in the
	order written, so graphics' text calls take a character the font has
	no glyph for from the first of them that has one. A family of the
	list is itself given the families after it. A family that is not
	installed, cannot be had at the height, or is in memory as a font a
	program added, is passed over; a font of the ROM's at the height is
	taken as it is, and ends the chain. The file is read at each call
	that makes a font, and a font in memory keeps the chain it was made
	with: a changed list is seen in fonts made after it.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: yes: for the disk, and for another task loading.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: no spinlock may be held.
	- Process: a Process, since it reads files.

   OWNERSHIP
	The font is the library's; the caller holds it open until
	`CloseFont`. Once nobody holds it, it stays loaded for the next caller
	until memory runs short. A font another one falls back to stays
	loaded for as long as that one, opened by anybody or not. A size
	rendered from an outline keeps the outline open - its font file
	in memory, one for all the sizes made from it - for as long as the
	size is loaded.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`graphics.library/OpenFont`, `graphics.library/WeighTAMatch`,
	`graphics.library/Text`, `AvailFonts`, `NewScaledDiskFont`

   EXAMPLES
	    const want = sdk.graphics.TextAttr{ .name = "spleen.font", .y_size = 16 };
	    const font = dfb.OpenDiskFont(&want) orelse return error.NoFont;
	    defer gb.CloseFont(font);
diskfont.library/OpenOutlineFont              diskfont.library/OpenOutlineFont

   NAME
	OpenOutlineFont -- A family's outline, for drawing it at any size and angle with truetype.library.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn OpenOutlineFont(dfb: *DiskfontBase, name: [*:0]const u8, style: graphics.FontStyle) ?*diskfont.OutlineFont

   SINCE
	1.4. LVO -40.

   INPUTS
	- `name` - the family's name, "sans.font", or a path to its contents
	  file.
	- `style` - the styles wanted: `FSF_BOLD` and `FSF_ITALIC` count,
	  the others are a program's own to draw.

   RESULT
	The outline, open: its `outline` for truetype.library's calls, and the
	styles its face is drawn in. Null if the family is not there, has no
	outline (a family of bitmap sizes only), or its file cannot be read
	or is no font truetype.library reads, or there is no memory.

   BEHAVIOR
	**The family's own face for the styles asked**, where one is
	installed beside it, as `OpenDiskFont` finds them: `sans-bolditalic.font`,
	`sans-bold.font`, `sans-italic.font` beside `sans.font`, each only for
	styles asked, the one drawing the most of them first; the family's
	own outline otherwise. `style` in the result says which styles the
	face is drawn in, so a program can draw the rest - lean it for an
	italic the family has no face of.

	The face's font file is read whole and opened once: everyone who
	asks for it is given the same, until the last has closed it. It then
	stays, for the next to ask, until memory runs short. truetype.library
	is opened the first time an outline is.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: yes: for the disk, and for another task loading a font.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: no spinlock may be held.
	- Process: a Process, since it reads files.

   OWNERSHIP
	The outline is the library's, shared; the caller holds it open until
	`CloseOutlineFont`, and keeps diskfont.library open as long. It does
	not call truetype.library's `CloseOutline` on it. The calls that read
	it - `GlyphSegments` and the rest - change nothing in it, so any
	number of tasks may read it at once.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`CloseOutlineFont`, `OpenDiskFont`, `truetype.library/GlyphSegments`

   EXAMPLES
	    const face = dfb.OpenOutlineFont("serif.font", sdk.graphics.FSF_ITALIC) orelse return error.NoFont;
	    defer dfb.CloseOutlineFont(face);
	    const glyph = tb.GlyphIndex(face.outline, 'g');
